= 1E
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Groups}

= i
{parent=1e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

A <transposition> exchanges exactly two letters and fixes the rest. A <permutation cycle> $(a_1\,a_2\,\ldots,a_k)$ sends each displayed letter to the next, sends $a_k$ to $a_1$, and fixes all other letters.

After imposing $\sigma(1)=2$, the remaining $n-1$ domain elements may be sent bijectively to the remaining $n-1$ values in arbitrary order. Hence the number is
$$
\boxed{(n-1)!}.
$$

= ii
{parent=1e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

There are $\lfloor n/2\rfloor$ even letters. The stated condition and injectivity force the even letters to permute among themselves, which can be done in $\lfloor n/2\rfloor!$ ways. The remaining odd letters must then permute among themselves. Thus the number is
$$
\boxed{\lfloor n/2\rfloor!\,\lceil n/2\rceil!}.
$$

= iii
{parent=1e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Choose the support of the four-cycle and then cyclically order it. A chosen set of four letters has $(4-1)!=6$ distinct cycles, so the answer is
$$
\boxed{6\binom n4}.
$$

= iv
{parent=1e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

The <product of two transpositions> has three possible forms. Equal transpositions produce the identity, transpositions meeting in one letter produce a <three-cycle>, and disjoint transpositions produce two disjoint two-cycles. There are two three-cycles on each chosen triple and three pairings of each chosen four-set. Therefore the number of distinct permutations representable this way is
$$
\boxed{1+2\binom n3+3\binom n4}.
$$

= 2D
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Numbers and Sets}

= i
{parent=2d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

An <equivalence relation> is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive. Here
$$
|x-y|^2<|x|^2+|y|^2
\iff \operatorname{Re}(x\overline y)>0.
$$
The relation is reflexive and symmetric, but it is not transitive. For example, take
$$
x=1,\qquad y=e^{i\pi/3},\qquad z=e^{2i\pi/3}.
$$
The real parts of $x\overline y$ and $y\overline z$ are positive, whereas that of $x\overline z$ is negative. Hence this is not an equivalence relation.

= ii
{parent=2d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

By the <equality condition in the complex triangle inequality>,
$$
|x+y|=|x|+|y|
$$
for nonzero $x,y$ exactly when $x/y$ is a positive real number. Having the same <complex argument> is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, so this is an equivalence relation. Its classes are the open rays from the origin.

= iii
{parent=2d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

This relation is reflexive because choosing $n=1$ gives $|x/x|=1$. It need not be symmetric: take positive real numbers $y=e$ and $x=e^2$. Then $|x/y^2|=1$ is rational, so $x\sim y$. But
$$
\left|\frac{y}{x^m}\right|=e^{1-2m}
$$
is transcendental, hence irrational, for every integer $m\geq1$. Thus $y\not\sim x$, and the relation is not an equivalence relation.

= iv
{parent=2d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Set $F(x)=|x^3-x|$. The relation is exactly
$$
x\sim y\iff F(x)=F(y).
$$
It is therefore the <equivalence relation induced by a function>: equality of the two function values is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive. Hence this is an equivalence relation.

= 3B
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Vector Calculus}

= a
{parent=3b}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Parametrize the straight segment by $x=t$, $y=1+t$, $0\leq t\leq1$. The <line integral> becomes
$$
\begin{aligned}
\int_0^1\bigl[t^2-(1+t)+(1+t)^2+t\bigr]\,dt
&=\int_0^1(2t^2+2t)\,dt\\
&=\boxed{\frac53}.
\end{aligned}
$$

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For the parabola, $x=t$, $y=1+t^2$, $dx=dt$, and $dy=2t\,dt$. Hence
$$
\begin{aligned}
\int_0^1\left[-1+
2t\bigl((1+t^2)^2+t\bigr)\right]dt
&=\int_0^1(-1+2t+2t^2+4t^3+2t^5)\,dt\\
&=\boxed{2}.
\end{aligned}
$$
The difference from the straight-path value reflects that the one-form is not exact.

= b
{parent=3b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

<Green theorem> states that for a positively oriented planar region $D$,
$$
\oint_{\partial D}P\,dx+Q\,dy
=\iint_D\left(\frac{\partial Q}{\partial x}
-\frac{\partial P}{\partial y}\right)dA.
$$
Here $P=xy^2-\lambda y$ and $Q=x^2y$, so
$$
Q_x-P_y=2xy-(2xy-\lambda)=\lambda.
$$
The stated orientations make $C_1\cup C_2$ the positive boundary of the region inside the radius-$a$ circle and outside the radius-$b$ circle. Its area is $\pi(a^2-b^2)$ regardless of the inner circle's center. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\int_{C_1\cup C_2}P\,dx+Q\,dy
=\pi\lambda(a^2-b^2)}.
$$

= 4C
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Dynamics and Relativity}

= i
{parent=4c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <central force> conserves <angular momentum>. The initial impact parameter gives $L=ub$, while at closest approach the velocity is transverse and $L=wp$. Thus $u=wp/b$. <Conservation of energy> gives
$$
\frac{u^2}{2}-\frac{k}{R}
=\frac{w^2}{2}-\frac{k}{p}.
$$
Under $u^2\gg2k/R$, neglect the initial potential term and substitute $u=wp/b$:
$$
\frac{w^2p^2}{2b^2}
=\frac{w^2}{2}-\frac{k}{p}.
$$
Consequently
$$
\boxed{\frac{w^2}{k}
=\frac{2b^2}{p(b^2-p^2)}}.
$$

= ii
{parent=4c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Now $E=u^2/2-k/R<0$, so the orbit is bound. Since $L=ub$, the initial energy is
$$
E=\frac{L^2}{2b^2}-\frac{k}{R}.
$$
At the farthest <central-force radial turning point> $q$, the radial speed vanishes and the transverse speed is $L/q$, giving
$$
E=\frac{L^2}{2q^2}-\frac{k}{q}.
$$
Equating these expressions and multiplying by $2q^2$ yields
$$
\boxed{\left(\frac{L^2}{b^2}-\frac{2k}{R}\right)q^2
+2kq-L^2=0}.
$$
**Thus $A=L^2/b^2-2k/R$, $B=2k$, and $C=-L^2$.**

= 5E
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Groups}

= i
{parent=5e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Write the <Möbius transformation> as
$$
f(z)=\frac{az+b}{cz+d},
\qquad ad-bc\ne0.
$$
A finite <fixed point of a Möbius transformation> satisfies
$$
cz^2+(d-a)z-b=0.
$$
If three distinct points of the Riemann sphere are fixed, this quadratic is identically zero, with the usual interpretation when one point is infinity. Hence $c=b=0$ and $d=a$, so $f(z)=z$. A nonidentity transformation's fixed-point equation is a nonzero polynomial of degree at most two on the sphere, and therefore it has one repeated fixed point or two distinct fixed points.

= ii
{parent=5e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

**True.** Conjugate the unique fixed point to infinity. The resulting map lies in the <affine subgroup of the Möbius group>, so it has the form $g(z)=az+b$. A unique fixed point forces $a=1$ and $b\ne0$; otherwise $g$ would have a finite fixed point as well. Conjugation by the scaling $z\mapsto z/b$ changes $z\mapsto z+b$ into $z\mapsto z+1$. Thus a nonidentity Möbius transformation with one fixed point is conjugate to a translation.

= iii
{parent=5e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

**True.** A Möbius transformation fixes infinity exactly when $c=0$, so it has the affine form
$$
z\longmapsto \alpha z+\beta,\qquad \alpha\ne0.
$$
This is the composition of the scaling $z\mapsto\alpha z$ and the translation $z\mapsto z+\beta$.

= iv
{parent=5e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

**False.** Compositions of scalings $z\mapsto\mu z$ and inversion $z\mapsto1/z$ reduce to one of the forms $\nu z$ or $\nu/z$; they preserve the unordered pair $\{0,\infty\}$. But
$$
f(z)=\frac{z}{z+1}
$$
is a <Möbius transformation> fixing $0$ and sends infinity to $1$, so it cannot be such a composition.

= 6E
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Groups}

= a
{parent=6e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The singleton orbits are exactly $X^G$. Every other orbit represented by $x$ has size $|G|/|G_x|$ by the <orbit-stabilizer theorem>. Partitioning $X$ into its <orbits of a group action> therefore gives
$$
|X|=|X^G|+\sum_x\frac{|G|}{|G_x|}.
$$

For the second identity, count
$$
Z=\{(g,x)\in G\times X:g\cdot x=x\}
$$
in two ways. Holding $g$ fixed gives
$$
|Z|=\sum_{g\in G}|X^g|.
$$
Holding $x$ fixed gives $|Z|=\sum_{x\in X}|G_x|$. Each orbit contributes $|G|/|G_x|$ points, each with stabilizer size $|G_x|$, and hence contributes $|G|$. Thus $|Z|=|G|\,|X/G|$, proving <Burnside lemma>:
$$
\boxed{|X/G|=\frac1{|G|}\sum_{g\in G}|X^g|}.
$$

= b
{parent=6e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For the rotation group $C_5$, the identity fixes all $n^5$ colorings. Because five is prime, each of the four nonidentity rotations is a single five-cycle and fixes exactly the $n$ constant colorings. <Burnside lemma> therefore gives the number of <coloring necklaces>
$$
\boxed{\frac{n^5+4n}{5}}.
$$

For the <dihedral group> $D_{10}$, the five reflections each fix one vertex and interchange two pairs of vertices, so each fixes $n^3$ colorings. Adding these to the identity and rotation contributions gives the number of <coloring bracelets>
$$
\boxed{\frac{n^5+5n^3+4n}{10}}.
$$

= 7D
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Numbers and Sets}

= a
{parent=7d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <Euler totient function> $\phi(n)$ is the number of residue classes modulo $n$ that are coprime to $n$. The <Chinese remainder theorem> says that for pairwise coprime moduli $m_i$, reduction induces a bijection
$$
\mathbb Z/(m_1\cdots m_r)\mathbb Z
\longrightarrow\prod_{i=1}^r\mathbb Z/m_i\mathbb Z.
$$
It restricts to a bijection of unit groups. If $n=p_1\cdots p_r$ is squarefree, then
$$
\boxed{\phi(n)=\prod_{i=1}^r(p_i-1)}.
$$
Each of the primes $3,5,17,257$ is one more than a power of two, so every squarefree product of them has power-of-two totient. Ten odd examples are
$$
\boxed{3,5,15,17,51,85,255,257,771,1285}.
$$

= b
{parent=7d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <Fermat-Euler theorem> states that if $\gcd(a,n)=1$, then
$$
\boxed{a^{\phi(n)}\equiv1\pmod n}.
$$
Let $r_1,\ldots,r_{\phi(n)}$ be a reduced residue system modulo $n$. Multiplication by $a$ permutes these classes, because $ar_i\equiv ar_j\pmod n$ implies $r_i\equiv r_j\pmod n$. Hence
$$
\prod_i ar_i\equiv\prod_i r_i\pmod n.
$$
Every $r_i$ is invertible modulo $n$, so cancellation gives $a^{\phi(n)}\equiv1\pmod n$.

= c
{parent=7d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

In the <RSA cryptosystem>, choose distinct large primes $p,q$, put $N=pq$, and choose $e$ coprime to
$$
\phi(N)=(p-1)(q-1).
$$
The decryption exponent is the <modular multiplicative inverse>
$$
d\equiv e^{-1}\pmod{\phi(N)}.
$$
Thus $ed=1+k\phi(N)$. For a message with $\gcd(m,N)=1$, the <Fermat-Euler theorem> gives
$$
c^d\equiv m^{ed}
=m(m^{\phi(N)})^k\equiv m\pmod N.
$$
For $N=187=11\cdot17$, $\phi(N)=160$. The <extended Euclidean algorithm> gives
$$
1=37\cdot13-3\cdot160,
$$
so
$$
\boxed{d=37}.
$$

= 8D
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Numbers and Sets}

= a
{parent=8d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

A set is <countable set>[countable] when it is finite or admits a bijection with a subset of $\mathbb N$; equivalently, its elements can be listed in a sequence. The integers are countable, for example in the order $0,1,-1,2,-2,\ldots$. Enumerating pairs by diagonals, or applying the <Cantor pairing function>, then shows that $\mathbb N\times\mathbb Z$ is countable.

Suppose instead that the <power set> $\mathcal P(\mathbb N)$ had an enumeration $A_1,A_2,\ldots$. The <Cantor diagonal argument> forms
$$
D=\{n\in\mathbb N:n\notin A_n\}.
$$
For every $n$, the set $D$ differs from $A_n$ at the element $n$, so it is absent from the alleged enumeration. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\mathcal P(\mathbb N)\text{ is uncountable}}.
$$

= b
{parent=8d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

If $x$ is a <fixed point> of $f$, then
$$
g(\sigma x)=\sigma f\sigma^{-1}(\sigma x)
=\sigma f(x)=\sigma x.
$$
Conversely, applying the same argument to $f=\sigma^{-1}g\sigma$ transports every fixed point of $g$ back to one of $f$. Thus the bijection $\sigma$ restricts to a bijection between the two fixed-point sets, so <conjugate functions> have the same number of fixed points.

= c
{parent=8d}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Choose a bijection $h:\mathbb N\to\mathbb Z$, and conjugate the translation of the integers:
$$
\boxed{f=h^{-1}\circ(z\mapsto z+1)\circ h}.
$$
Then $f$ is a bijection of $\mathbb N$. Its $k$th <iteration of a map>[iterate] is $h^{-1}(z\mapsto z+k)h$, which has no fixed point for every $k\geq1$. Hence $f\in T$.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The set $T$ is uncountable. Partition $\mathbb N$ into countably many infinite sets $B_1,B_2,\ldots$, and choose bijections $h_j:B_j\to\mathbb Z$. For every subset $A\subseteq\mathbb N$, define a permutation $f_A$ separately on each block by
$$
h_j(f_A(x))=
\begin{cases}
h_j(x)+1,&j\in A,\\
h_j(x)-1,&j\notin A.
\end{cases}
$$
Every positive iterate translates each integer coordinate by a nonzero amount, so it has no fixed point and $f_A\in T$. Distinct subsets $A$ give distinct permutations, yielding an injection $\mathcal P(\mathbb N)\hookrightarrow T$. Since the <power set> is uncountable,
$$
\boxed{T\text{ is uncountable}}.
$$
In fact its cardinality is the continuum, because it is a subset of all functions $\mathbb N\to\mathbb N$.

= 9B
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Vector Calculus}

= a
{parent=9b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The surface is an annular band of the downward-opening paraboloid, bounded by the circles $r=1,z=4$ and $r=2,z=1$. Choose the upward orientation.

\Image[paper-2-paraboloid-annulus.png]
{height=580}

The <curl> of $\mathbf A=(3y,-xz,yz^2)$ is
$$
\nabla\times\mathbf A=(z^2+x,0,-z-3).
$$
For the graph $z=5-x^2-y^2$, the upward vector <surface integral> element is
$$
d\mathbf S=(2x,2y,1)\,dx\,dy.
$$
Over the annulus $1<r<2$, the odd term $2xz^2$ integrates to zero. Therefore
$$
\begin{aligned}
I&=\iint_{1<r<2}(2x^2-z-3)\,dx\,dy\\
&=\int_1^2\int_0^{2\pi}
(2r^2\cos^2\theta+r^2-8)r\,d\theta\,dr\\
&=\int_1^2(4\pi r^3-16\pi r)\,dr
=\boxed{-9\pi}.
\end{aligned}
$$

= b
{parent=9b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

<Stokes theorem> gives
$$
I=\oint_{\partial S}\mathbf A\mathbin\cdot d\mathbf r.
$$
For the upward orientation, the outer circle $r=2,z=1$ is counterclockwise from above and the inner circle $r=1,z=4$ is clockwise. On a counterclockwise circle of radius $R$ and height $z$,
$$
\oint\mathbf A\mathbin\cdot d\mathbf r
=-R^2\int_0^{2\pi}(3\sin^2\theta+z\cos^2\theta)\,d\theta
=-\pi R^2(3+z).
$$
The outer circle contributes $-16\pi$. The inner circle would contribute $-7\pi$ counterclockwise and hence contributes $+7\pi$ with its induced clockwise orientation. Thus
$$
\boxed{I=-16\pi+7\pi=-9\pi},
$$
agreeing with the direct calculation.

= 10B
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Vector Calculus}

= a
{parent=10b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

In <suffix notation>,
$$
\boxed{\frac{\partial x_i}{\partial x_j}=\delta_{ij}},
$$
where $\delta_{ij}$ is the <Kronecker delta>. Since $r=(x_kx_k)^{1/2}$, the <chain rule> gives
$$
\boxed{\frac{\partial r}{\partial x_j}=\frac{x_j}{r}}.
$$

= b
{parent=10b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Writing $a_kx_k=\mathbf a\mathbin\cdot\mathbf x$ and differentiating gives
$$
d_{ij}=-\frac{a_ix_j}{r^3}
+\frac{a_jx_i}{r^3}
+\frac{(\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf x)\delta_{ij}}{r^3}
-\frac{3(\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf x)x_ix_j}{r^5}.
$$
Its <symmetric second-rank tensor> part and <antisymmetric second-rank tensor> part are
$$
d_{(ij)}=\frac{(\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf x)\delta_{ij}}{r^3}
-\frac{3(\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf x)x_ix_j}{r^5},
\qquad
d_{[ij]}=\frac{a_jx_i-a_ix_j}{r^3}.
$$
Taking the trace makes the two symmetric terms cancel, so the <divergence> is
$$
\boxed{\partial_i u_i=0}.
$$
Contracting the antisymmetric part with the Levi-Civita symbol gives the <curl>
$$
\boxed{\nabla\times\mathbf u
=\frac{2\,\mathbf a\times\mathbf x}{r^3}}.
$$

= c
{parent=10b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Suppose $u_1,u_2$ are solutions and put $w=u_1-u_2$. Then $\Delta w=0$ and $w$ satisfies the homogeneous <Robin boundary condition>
$$
(1-\lambda)w+\lambda\frac{\partial w}{\partial n}=0.
$$
For $0<\lambda<1$, <Green's first identity> gives
$$
\int_V|\nabla w|^2\,dV
=\int_Sw\frac{\partial w}{\partial n}\,dS
=-\frac{1-\lambda}{\lambda}\int_Sw^2\,dS\leq0.
$$
The left side is nonnegative, so both sides vanish and $w=0$. For $\lambda=0$, the <Dirichlet boundary condition> $w=0$ on $S$ and the same identity again imply $w=0$. The solution is therefore unique for every $0\leq\lambda<1$.

For a regular spherically symmetric solution, the <Laplacian> equation is
$$
\frac1{r^2}\frac d{dr}\left(r^2u'\right)=6.
$$
Regularity at the origin gives $u'=2r$, hence $u=r^2+C$. Applying the boundary condition at $r=b$ yields
$$
\boxed{u(r)=r^2-b^2-\frac{2\lambda b}{1-\lambda}},
\qquad 0\leq\lambda<1.
$$
At $\lambda=1$ the <Neumann boundary condition> would require $u'(b)=2b=0$, which is impossible for $b>0$. Equivalently, its compatibility condition would require $\int_V6\,dV=0$.

= 11C
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Dynamics and Relativity}

= Solution
{parent=11C}

Let the heavier block move downward, so no slip gives linear acceleration $a\dot\omega$. Newton's equations and the pulley <torque> equation are
$$
T_1-Mg=Ma\dot\omega,\qquad
2Mg-T_2=2Ma\dot\omega,
$$
$$
(T_2-T_1)a-\lambda M\omega=qMa^2\dot\omega.
$$
Eliminating the tensions gives the first-order equation
$$
\boxed{(q+3)a^2\dot\omega+\lambda\omega=ga}.
$$
With $\omega(0)=0$, its solution is
$$
\boxed{\omega(t)=\frac{ga}{\lambda}
\left(1-e^{-\lambda t/[(q+3)a^2]}\right)}.
$$

For a thin slice of the solid of revolution, $dM=\rho\pi b(x)^2dx$ and its axial <moment of inertia> is $dI=\tfrac12b(x)^2dM$. Consequently
$$
q=\frac{I}{Ma^2}
=\frac12\frac{\int_{-1}^1(1+|x|)^4dx}
{\int_{-1}^1(1+|x|)^2dx}
=\frac12\frac{62/5}{14/3}
=\boxed{\frac{93}{70}}.
$$

= 12C
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Dynamics and Relativity}

= a
{parent=12c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Let the photon energies be $E_1,E_2$ and their momentum angle be $\theta$. Conservation of <four-momentum> and the null photon momenta give
$$
M^2c^2=(p_1+p_2)^2
=2p_1\mathbin\cdot p_2
=\frac{2E_1E_2}{c^2}(1-\cos\theta).
$$
Using $1-\cos\theta=2\sin^2(\theta/2)$,
$$
\boxed{\sin\frac\theta2
=\frac{Mc^2}{2\sqrt{E_1E_2}}}.
$$
**Thus the opening angle depends only on the parent rest energy and the two photon energies.**

= b
{parent=12c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

In the parent's rest frame, momentum conservation makes the massive daughter and photon momenta equal and opposite, of magnitude $p$. The <relativistic two-body decay> formulas give
$$
p=\frac{M^2-m^2}{2M}c,\qquad
E_R=\frac{M^2+m^2}{2M}c^2.
$$
Since $v=pc^2/E_R$ and $r=m/M$,
$$
\boxed{v=cf(r),\qquad f(r)=\frac{1-r^2}{1+r^2}}.
$$
The daughter's <Lorentz factor> is
$$
\gamma=\frac{E_R}{mc^2}=\frac{1+r^2}{2r}.
$$
If it decays after <proper time> $\tau$, <time dilation> gives $t=\gamma\tau$, while uniform motion gives $x=v\gamma\tau$. Therefore the event coordinates in the parent frame are
$$
\boxed{(ct,x)=
\left(\frac{1+r^2}{2r}c\tau,\,
\frac{1-r^2}{2r}c\tau\right)}.
$$
